<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Cordango.SourceGen</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The generator SDK. It knows what a generator IS; it knows nothing about any particular one.

    Everything here is about the contract between the CLI and a target: what a generator is handed,
    what it may answer, how a target says what it cannot do, and how files reach the disk safely and
    identically every time. `Cordango.SourceGen.DotNetVue` is a consumer of this project like any
    third-party generator would be, and if anything in here starts naming ASP.NET, Vue or EF Core,
    the boundary has already gone.

    One ProjectReference: the compiler, for the definition vocabulary a capability check has to speak
    (block kinds, effect types, field types all come out of the schema rather than out of a list
    somebody retypes here).
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Cordango.Compiler\Cordango.Compiler.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
